The names a modern application binds before main, and a bundle lookup that searches.

iA Writer is refused by the loader over five Foundation names it never calls at startup, because
dyld binds every one of them before the first instruction runs: the three
NSMetadataQueryDidUpdateNotification userInfo keys, NSUserActivityDocumentURLKey and
NSMultipleUnderlyingErrorsKey (declared here, defined in the CoreFoundation pin next to
NSUnderlyingErrorKey). NSEdgeInsetsEqual is a different case: declared in NSGeometry.h since 10.10
and never defined, so comparing two insets was a link error.

AND ONE REAL BUG, which is what actually got the application past its own +load: +[NSBundle
bundleWithIdentifier:] only looked in a table of bundles somebody had already created, with a TODO
saying it should search. A framework asking for ITSELF by identifier is ordinary -- iA Writer does
it from a +load through +[NSBundle(InterfaceFoundation) interfaceFoundationBundle], which is
[NSBundle bundleWithIdentifier: @"net.ia.InterfaceFoundation"] -- and it got nil, then inserted that
nil into an NSMutableSet, which raised before any window existed. +allFrameworks already walks every
loaded image and registers each framework it finds, so asking it once and looking again answers.

